WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.821
Every IVF index in FAISS starts as twenty-five full passes of k-means.

00:00:04.821 --> 00:00:07.341
What if two or three were enough?

00:00:10.793 --> 00:00:11.779
Hi, I'm Wrenly!

00:00:11.779 --> 00:00:14.737
This is e-means, from Meta FAIR, now in FAISS.

00:00:14.737 --> 00:00:17.695
Watch the two algorithms move, and you'll see why.

00:00:19.117 --> 00:00:21.550
This is Lloyd's algorithm, on the authors' toy.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.198
Assign every point to its nearest centroid, then move each centroid all

00:00:25.198 --> 00:00:27.631
the way to the mean of its points.

00:00:28.011 --> 00:00:31.155
One move per full pass, and the loss only goes down.

00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:33.156
So a bad start never gets fixed.

00:00:33.156 --> 00:00:34.013
It gets polished.

00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:37.049
e-means changes one thing.

00:00:37.049 --> 00:00:40.897
It keeps a moving average of each cluster's count and sum, and

00:00:40.897 --> 00:00:42.820
takes many small steps per pass.

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.595
Here is the whole update, as one step.

00:00:48.015 --> 00:00:51.652
Each update moves a centroid toward its cluster's mean in this batch.

00:00:52.072 --> 00:00:54.934
How far depends on the step, and on this batch's count

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:55.974
over the faded count.

00:00:56.394 --> 00:00:59.481
Make the batch the whole dataset, with a step of one, and

00:00:59.481 --> 00:01:00.510
you get Lloyd back.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.311
Lloyd is one corner of this family.

00:01:03.742 --> 00:01:05.288
Same points, same bad start.

00:01:05.288 --> 00:01:09.615
Each mini-batch moves a centroid a fraction of the way toward its batch mean.

00:01:09.995 --> 00:01:12.650
A batch is a sample, so the centroids jitter.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.715
That noise pulls the crowded pair apart.

00:01:16.575 --> 00:01:19.234
Side by side, in this film's own simulation.

00:01:19.234 --> 00:01:21.228
Five blobs, and one bad start.

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.681
Lloyd converges fast, and stays stuck.

00:01:27.148 --> 00:01:31.184
e-means takes small noisy steps, and a centroid finds the empty blob.

00:01:33.648 --> 00:01:36.471
Many small steps found what one exact step could not.

00:01:38.658 --> 00:01:42.325
The article has a playground that runs both for real.

00:01:42.325 --> 00:01:45.626
Sixteen hundred points, twenty-four centroids, the same random start.

00:01:46.006 --> 00:01:46.612
Press play.

00:01:46.612 --> 00:01:49.035
Lloyd, on the left, moves once per pass.

00:01:49.035 --> 00:01:51.458
e-means, on the right, is done after three.

00:01:51.838 --> 00:01:55.719
Six hundred small updates against Lloyd's twenty-five, and the blue curve ends

00:01:55.719 --> 00:01:57.336
just under the red one.

00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:00.620
It is not a clean sweep.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.418
Step to other seeds, and sometimes the ratio under the chart turns red.

00:02:04.418 --> 00:02:05.587
Lloyd wins that one.

00:02:07.392 --> 00:02:08.747
So run all forty seeds.

00:02:10.392 --> 00:02:13.855
At three epochs, e-means ends below Lloyd on twenty-eight of them.

00:02:14.449 --> 00:02:15.868
At one epoch, only ten.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:19.840
The laws are being stretched here, far below the sizes they were fitted on.

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:25.398
Flip revival off, and it wins twenty-five seeds instead of twenty-eight.

00:02:25.398 --> 00:02:27.935
On this toy that is a small effect.

00:02:28.315 --> 00:02:30.130
At real scale it is not.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.760
On a million points, four thousand three hundred and thirteen clusters die

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.878
without it, and forty-seven with the split.

00:02:37.392 --> 00:02:38.572
And nobody tunes it.

00:02:38.572 --> 00:02:40.637
Take a million points and three epochs.

00:02:41.037 --> 00:02:45.319
A fitted law picks the batch: sixty-five thousand five hundred and thirty-six.

00:02:45.719 --> 00:02:48.778
A second picks the peak step, zero point three six one.

00:02:49.178 --> 00:02:51.594
That is forty-eight updates, where Lloyd makes three.

00:02:51.994 --> 00:02:54.456
About four points per cluster each time.

00:02:54.456 --> 00:02:57.621
Sixteen noisy moves per pass, against one exact one.

00:02:59.575 --> 00:03:00.665
The paper's curves.

00:03:00.665 --> 00:03:02.844
One is Lloyd at twenty-five iterations.

00:03:02.844 --> 00:03:06.113
Blue, e-means, drops below it within a few epochs.

00:03:06.493 --> 00:03:09.301
The orange mini-batch baselines stall above the line.

00:03:09.301 --> 00:03:13.865
Across the datasets, e-means needs one point four to two point six epochs.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:16.183
Two cautions.

00:03:16.183 --> 00:03:18.649
The loss gain is fractions of a percent.

00:03:18.649 --> 00:03:19.882
The story is compute.

00:03:20.302 --> 00:03:24.073
And the laws were fitted on high-dimensional embeddings, not on tiny data.

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:28.085
Lloyd was the costliest corner of a bigger family.

00:03:28.085 --> 00:03:32.155
Many small, noisy, scheduled steps get there in a tenth of the passes.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.101
To recap.

00:03:35.101 --> 00:03:38.256
A moving average, small noisy steps, and fitted laws.

00:03:38.256 --> 00:03:39.308
Thanks for watching!

00:03:39.728 --> 00:03:41.829
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:41.829 --> 00:03:42.429
I'm Wrenly.

00:03:42.429 --> 00:03:42.729
Bye!
